Title:
ML Evasion in Packers/Loaders (RustPack v1.7) – Passive ML (EMBER) and Potential Active LLM Analysis

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This post explains how modern AV/EDR “machine-learning detections” often rely on passive PE metadata features (e.g., imports, sections, signing) rather than deep semantic code understanding, and how packer/loader builders can adapt.  
- It outlines minimum packer/loader features for long-term usability (polymorphism, string obfuscation, emulation bypass considerations, optional encryption) and why these alone still trigger ML classifiers.  
- Using EMBER2024 (a public PE malware ML dataset/model), the author demonstrates offline/automated scoring of generated loaders and shows that adding benign-looking IAT imports can drastically reduce malicious scores without changing core behavior.  
- Additional metadata tweaks that reduce EMBER scores include valid code signing, adding export table entries, and increasing `.pdata`/function table complexity.  
- The post also explores “active LLM analysis” as a future cloud-analysis technique, describing an iterative loop where an LLM reverse-engineers binaries (Capstone-based harness), produces IoC reports, and the loader is modified to remove/blur those IoCs.  
- It’s primarily useful for red team toolsmiths/malware developers building loaders/packers and for defenders seeking to understand weaknesses and evasion paths in metadata-driven ML detections.

Technical Focus:
- PE metadata-based ML detection (EMBER2024 feature space)
- Import Address Table (IAT) camouflage / benign import pumping
- Polymorphic loader generation and junk-code randomization
- String obfuscation strategies and anti-analysis tradeoffs
- Emulation engine behavior and bypass concepts (resource exhaustion / implementation breaks)
- LLM-assisted reverse engineering and iterative obfuscation loops (Capstone harness)

Use Cases:
- Offline testing of loader/packer outputs against EMBER-like ML classifiers
- Reducing static ML scores via import engineering, signing, and PE structure shaping
- Designing polymorphic loaders that avoid stable IoCs across builds
- Evaluating how quickly LLMs can extract IoCs from binaries and hardening against that workflow
- Backdooring legitimate binaries while attempting to preserve benign “first impressions” in analysis

Keywords:
RustPack, packer, loader, ML evasion, EMBER2024, passive metadata analysis, PE features, Import Address Table, IAT camouflage, Win32 API imports, code signing certificate, export address table, .pdata, entropy, string obfuscation, emulation engine, Capstone, LLM reverse engineering, IoC removal, polymorphism